window.KillerBenchSearch=[{"u":"help/index.html","g":"Hướng dẫn sử dụng","t":"Trợ giúp KillerBench","h":[["what-it-does","Ứng dụng làm gì"],["where-to-start","Bắt đầu từ đâu"]],"x":" Trợ giúp KillerBench  Các hướng dẫn chi tiết được liên kết từ trang này bằng tiếng Anh. \n KillerBench là bộ công cụ chẩn đoán và sửa chữa Windows dành cho kỹ thuật viên. Nó kiểm tra máy tính trước, giải thích kết quả cùng bằng chứng cho từng phát hiện và chỉ thay đổi Windows khi bạn chọn một cách sửa cụ thể rồi xác nhận. \n KillerBench đang được phát triển. Chưa có bản tải xuống công khai. Những trang này mô tả bản dựng hiện tại. \n Ứng dụng làm gì \n  Chạy chẩn đoán chỉ đọc cho Windows Update, thiết bị, lưu trữ, mạng, in ấn, bảo mật, ứng dụng và hồ sơ người dùng, sau đó tạo báo cáo dễ đọc.  Cung cấp các thao tác sửa chữa có kiểm soát, chỉ chạy khi bằng chứng mới xác nhận đúng vấn đề và xác minh kết quả sau đó.  Bao gồm thiết lập và tăng cường bảo mật như BitLocker, cài đặt quyền riêng tư, dọn dẹp Windows và chuẩn bị máy tính mới.  Chạy chẩn đoán và phân tích báo cáo cục bộ, không cần AI hay tài khoản KillerBench. Những thao tác được chọn như tải xuống và sao lưu khóa khôi phục vào Entra ID có thể kết nối với dịch vụ bên ngoài.  Dùng giao diện ứng dụng đang dùng cho bảng điều khiển, báo cáo, xác nhận và hộp thoại Mở, Lưu, Thư mục.  \n Bắt đầu từ đâu \n   Lần chẩn đoán đầu tiên  hướng dẫn từng bước quét toàn bộ hệ thống.   Không gian làm việc  giải thích các mục, bảng và phím tắt.   Cách giữ an toàn khi thay đổi  giải thích những gì KillerBench kiểm tra trước và sau mỗi thay đổi.  Nhóm trang  Các mục  mô tả từng công cụ, được nhóm giống như trong ứng dụng.   Dòng lệnh  liệt kê mọi lệnh  killerbench-cli  được hỗ trợ.   Khắc phục sự cố  trình bày các thao tác bị chặn, khởi động lại và yêu cầu bị gián đoạn.  \n Nhà phát triển muốn tích hợp chẩn đoán vào công cụ của mình có thể đọc  Hướng dẫn nhà phát triển Engine . "},{"u":"engine/index.html","g":"Hướng dẫn nhà phát triển","t":"KillerBench.Engine","h":[["what-it-provides","Ứng dụng cung cấp gì"],["designed-for-traceability","Được thiết kế để truy vết"],["diagnostics-first","Chẩn đoán trước"],["desktop-host","Ứng dụng máy tính"],["current-status","Trạng thái hiện tại"],["choose-a-guide","Chọn hướng dẫn"]],"x":" KillerBench.Engine  Các hướng dẫn chi tiết được liên kết từ trang này bằng tiếng Anh. \n KillerBench.Engine là thư viện chẩn đoán và sửa chữa Windows dành cho ứng dụng cần bằng chứng có cấu trúc thay vì tập hợp các tập lệnh khó hiểu. Thư viện vận hành các công cụ KillerBench trên máy tính và dòng lệnh, đồng thời có thể tích hợp vào một ứng dụng .NET khác. \n Ứng dụng cung cấp gì \n  Quy trình chẩn đoán có phạm vi xác định cho bảo trì Windows, thiết bị, lưu trữ, mạng, in ấn, bảo mật, ứng dụng, hồ sơ người dùng và các khu vực hệ thống khác  Bằng chứng có kiểu dữ liệu và trạng thái rõ ràng: đầy đủ, chưa đầy đủ, chưa biết và trong quá khứ  Kịch bản sửa chữa cần bằng chứng chính xác, hiện tại trước khi chạy  Nhật ký được lưu bền vững để xác minh sửa chữa và khôi phục lượt chạy bị gián đoạn  Hàm gọi lại tiến độ cho hiển thị trực tiếp, chẳng hạn hoạt động bảo trì hệ thống  Văn bản, JSON, HTML và kết quả .NET có cấu trúc cho báo cáo và tích hợp  Gói kiến thức có phiên bản cho mã lỗi, định danh phần cứng, nhà cung cấp và tài liệu tham khảo hỗ trợ  \n Được thiết kế để truy vết \n Mỗi phát hiện đều gắn với bằng chứng đã tạo ra nó. Sự kiện trong quá khứ vẫn là sự kiện trong quá khứ. Thiếu dữ liệu không có nghĩa là hệ thống hoạt động tốt. Lỗi của bộ cung cấp dữ liệu không được coi là kết quả trống. Engine báo rõ phạm vi đã kiểm tra để ứng dụng sử dụng nó có thể giải thích điều gì đã biết và điều gì chưa biết. \n Chẩn đoán trước \n Phần lớn thao tác Engine chỉ đọc dữ liệu. Phạm vi sửa chữa được chủ ý giới hạn hơn phạm vi chẩn đoán. Một thao tác sửa chữa phải có kịch bản được hỗ trợ, bằng chứng mới về điều kiện thực hiện, sự cho phép rõ ràng từ ứng dụng sử dụng Engine, bản ghi chuẩn bị được lưu bền vững, thao tác hệ thống có phạm vi xác định và xác minh sau khi thực hiện. \n Quét toàn bộ hệ thống chỉ thực hiện chẩn đoán, kể cả khi một quy trình cụ thể đã bật sửa chữa tự động. Bản chụp đã lưu và sự kiện trong quá khứ không bao giờ cho phép sửa chữa. Việc kiểm chứng sửa chữa hệ thống vẫn giới hạn trong bằng chứng được lưu từ các máy Windows dùng cho thử nghiệm, ghi trong tệp sau của kho mã:  VM-VALIDATION.md . \n Ứng dụng máy tính \n Giao diện máy tính trình bày đầu ra Engine qua các chế độ Đầu ra thô và Báo cáo có thể đọc được. Các xác nhận và hộp thoại Mở, Lưu, Thư mục theo giao diện ứng dụng đang dùng. Những thành phần này thuộc ứng dụng máy tính; ứng dụng tích hợp Engine phải cung cấp giao diện xem xét và cho phép riêng, đồng thời giữ nguyên các điều kiện về bằng chứng, điều kiện tiên quyết, nhật ký và xác minh của Engine. \n Trạng thái hiện tại \n Engine hướng đến .NET 10 và Windows. Engine đang được phát triển tích cực và hiện được sử dụng thông qua tham chiếu dự án trong kho mã KillerBench. Chưa phát hành gói công khai. \n Chọn hướng dẫn \n   Bắt đầu  minh họa quy trình chỉ đọc đầu tiên.   Quy trình chẩn đoán  giải thích các kiểm tra cụ thể và quét toàn bộ hệ thống.   Bằng chứng và kết quả  giải thích trạng thái, nhật ký, tiến độ và báo cáo.   Sửa chữa và an toàn  mô tả các điều kiện về bằng chứng và mô hình khôi phục.   Hướng dẫn CLI  trình bày cách tìm lệnh, đầu ra văn bản và đầu ra JSON.   Hướng dẫn API  giới thiệu các điểm truy cập công khai chính.   Kiến trúc  giải thích ranh giới Engine và các lớp bên trong.   Gói kiến thức  trình bày dữ liệu tham khảo có thể cập nhật.  "},{"u":"../help/first-run.html","g":"User guide","t":"Your first diagnosis","h":[["run-the-scan","Run the scan"],["read-the-report","Read the report"],["choose-checks-yourself","Choose checks yourself"],["fix-what-you-found","Fix what you found"]],"x":"Your first diagnosis A full system scan is the fastest way to learn what is wrong with a computer. It only reads, so it is safe to run on any machine. Run the scan 1. Open KillerBench. The Diagnostics section opens with Full system scan selected. 2. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues turned off for your first run. 3. Press Run diagnosis . Progress appears in the console while each check runs. 4. When it finishes, KillerBench switches to the Readable report . Run KillerBench as administrator when you can. Some Windows evidence, such as BitLocker protectors and protected services, is only readable with administrator rights. Without them those checks report that the evidence was unavailable instead of guessing. Read the report The report starts with counts for Needs review , Verified repairs , Incomplete and Checked , then a recommended next step. - What needs attention lists problems the evidence supports, most important first. - Collected information shows what was read, including evidence that was incomplete or unavailable. - Historical events are labeled as history. An error from last month is not treated as a current fault. Missing evidence never counts as healthy. If a check could not run, the report says so. Choose checks yourself Open Choose checks (advanced) to pick individual checks instead of the full scan. Each section in the app also has focused tools, described in the Sections pages of this guide. Fix what you found When the report recommends a fix, it names the section and tool to open. Read How changes stay safe before your first repair."},{"u":"../help/workspace.html","g":"User guide","t":"The workspace","h":[["sections","Sections"],["tool-sidebar","Tool sidebar"],["evidence-console","Evidence console"],["inspector-and-activity-log","Inspector and activity log"],["themes-languages-and-shortcuts","Themes, languages and shortcuts"]],"x":"The workspace The KillerBench window has four areas: the section buttons, the tool sidebar, the evidence console and the activity log. Sections The buttons across the top group every tool into eleven sections: Diagnostics, Repair, Updates, Devices, Network, Apps, Maintenance, Onboarding, Security, BitLocker and Privacy. Pick a section, then pick a tool from the list in the sidebar. Tool sidebar The sidebar on the left shows the selected section, its tool list and the options for the chosen tool. Options that could change the machine are always off by default. Press the run button to start the tool. Stop cancels the current run. Evidence console The main pane has two views. - Raw output shows the technical evidence as it is collected, with original line numbers. - Readable report shows the finished report for the last run. Use the filter box to show only matching lines. Export report saves the report with its source paths and evidence excerpts. Session sources lists every piece of evidence gathered in this session. Inspector and activity log The inspector on the right opens for repairs and evidence details. The activity log at the bottom records what KillerBench did in this session. Themes, languages and shortcuts The rail between the sidebar and the console holds the theme picker, the language picker, the shortcut list and the activity log toggle. KillerBench has 13 themes and 19 languages. The smoked-glass console and readable reports follow the active theme. Confirmations and Open, Save, and Folder dialogs use the same family styling, including the 98SE theme. Choose the theme before opening a dialog to use that appearance throughout the workflow. Key Action --- --- F1 Keyboard shortcuts F2 Show or hide the tool sidebar F3 Show or hide the activity log F12 About KillerBench"},{"u":"../help/safety.html","g":"User guide","t":"How changes stay safe","h":[["before-a-change","Before a change"],["after-a-change","After a change"],["if-something-is-interrupted","If something is interrupted"],["what-killerbench-will-not-do","What KillerBench will not do"],["development-status","Development status"]],"x":"How changes stay safe KillerBench treats diagnosis and change as different jobs. Diagnosis only reads. The full system scan never runs repairs. Select a specific repair or action, review its options and prerequisites, and confirm the proposed change. Before a change 1. Fresh evidence. The tool reads the current state again. A saved report or an old event never authorizes a change. 2. Exact eligibility. The change is offered only when the evidence matches the exact problem it fixes. Anything incomplete blocks it. 3. Your confirmation. Review the selected action in its confirmation dialog. Some actions, such as decrypting a drive, need a second confirmation. The dialog follows the active app theme; its appearance does not change the action's requirements. 4. Prerequisites. Administrator rights, restart state and ownership by policy or device management are checked. Settings that a policy owns are left alone. 5. A pending record. KillerBench writes down what it is about to do before it asks Windows to do it. 6. A final recheck. Everything is checked again immediately before the request, so a change in the meantime stops it. After a change - KillerBench reads the result back and reports Verified only when Windows shows the expected state. - If Windows returns success but the state did not change, the result says so and nothing is repeated. - Each change is saved in a journal under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench . If something is interrupted If KillerBench is closed or loses contact while a request may have reached Windows, the pending record stays. The next run of that action checks what actually happened instead of sending the request again. The command line has matching -recover commands for the same purpose. What KillerBench will not do - Clear a TPM, delete protectors it did not create, or display recovery passwords. - Change a setting that group policy or device management controls. - Run a repair just because a historical event or imported report mentions a problem. Diagnostics and report analysis run locally. An action you select can contact an external destination, including software downloads or recovery-key backup to Active Directory or Entra ID. Review the target before confirming. Saved captures and raw evidence can include machine identity and source paths, so review them before sharing. Development status Repair coverage is still expanding. A successful build or simulated check does not establish native repair behavior. The repository's VM-VALIDATION.md tracks disposable-Windows-machine scenarios and remaining validation."},{"u":"../help/diagnostics.html","g":"User guide","t":"Diagnostics","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Diagnostics Diagnostics is where every visit should start. Each tool reads the machine and writes a report. Nothing here changes Windows. The default tool, the full system scan, runs a broad set of read-only checks and ranks what needs attention. Tools in this section Check the machine first, then read the report for what needs attention and the next safe step. Full system scan Inspect 49 diagnostic areas, then evaluate every installed service-recovery recipe together with the servicing, DHCP, printing, Defender, time and WMI recipes. Checks can take several minutes. Auto-repair is optional and requires each recipe's own current evidence, machine prerequisites and dependency checks. Eligible repairs can renew missing DHCP leases, release queued print jobs, resynchronize the clock, refresh Defender signatures, restore eligible services and repair confirmed Windows corruption. Automatic stopped services may be started after their startup window and dependencies are verified. A stopped demand-start service remains unchanged when idle state is the only evidence; narrowly focused workflows can require it only when current feature evidence supports the start. Startup types, task definitions, reports, dumps, subscriptions, credentials, channel settings, firewall policy and managed settings are preserved. Historical, backup and transfer snapshots are read-only. Dump contents are not opened. Dirty-bit inspection does not schedule repair or restart. This workflow does not install updates, change drivers, delete files or perform a complete hardware test. Vitals Capture available memory, provider-reported disk health and BitLocker protection status, then correlate current vitals with device problems, reliability history, hardware-error records, crash evidence, unexpected shutdowns and Windows Memory Diagnostic results. Restart indicators, system disk free space, elevation and update service configuration remain visible. Recovery passwords are not collected. No repairs run in this workflow. The combined result is not a complete hardware, software or stability test. Reliability history Read the last 14 days of Windows Reliability Monitor records together with recent Application errors and unexpected shutdown events, then return one bounded historical conclusion. Historical records do not establish current health or root cause. No repair is selected and no applications, services or settings are changed. Startup performance Read the last 14 days of Windows startup and shutdown performance event metadata, retaining only provider identity and bounded numeric duration fields, then correlate it with supported pending restart indicators. Paths, process names, accounts and other event payload are removed before evidence is saved. Historical degradation records do not identify a current bottleneck or authorize startup, service, application, driver, task, power, restart or policy changes. Hardware error history Read the last 14 days of Windows Hardware Error Architecture and Memory Diagnostic history, current Plug and Play device problems, physical-disk health and provider reliability counters, then return one bounded hardware conclusion. Records require correlation with their payload, firmware diagnostics and vendor hardware tests. No failing component or root cause is inferred, no repair is selected, and no hardware, firmware, driver, disk, pool, volume, file, service, power, restart or setting is changed. Driver installation history Read the retained tail of Windows SetupAPI device-install history, correlate failed sections with exact current Plug and Play identities and return one bounded conclusion. Historical log sections do not prove a device is still present or a failure is current. No driver is installed, removed, rolled back or updated. System Restore points List up to 256 available System Restore points, review explicit creation eligibility and return one bounded readiness conclusion. The inventory does not prove that protection is enabled for every volume or that a restore will succeed. No restore point is created, deleted or applied during diagnosis. Windows Recovery Environment Read the current Windows Recovery Environment configuration with REAgentC, correlate an enabled local location with the exact current disk and partition, and return one bounded readiness conclusion. Optional automatic repair can enable a disabled existing configuration, then verify its reported state. The recovery image is not downloaded, relocated or replaced, no recovery boot is initiated, and Windows is not restarted. Enabled status and partition presence do not prove that the recovery image can boot or repair this PC. Windows activation Read current Windows license status without collecting full or partial product keys, retain the last seven days of sanitized Software Protection warning, error and critical event metadata, then return one bounded operational conclusion. Event payload is removed before evidence is saved. Historical records remain context when current licensing reports Licensed. No activation, product-key, token, license, account, service or policy action is selected. Provider status and event history do not prove subscription entitlement, activation-server reachability, token-store integrity, future validity or root cause. Storage Map bounded disk, partition and drive-letter volume identities, evaluate 10% free-space pressure across every drive-letter volume, inspect VSS shadow-storage allocation and per-volume quota defaults, summarize current Storage module job progress, check the selected NTFS volume and its online self-healing state, and inspect disk/controller devices across this PC. Capacity thresholds, quota settings and job states are review evidence and do not select cleanup, resizing, quota changes or job control. Drive-letter mapping is point-in-time and does not cover every mount path. Device findings are not mapped to the selected volume. The Windows volume is the default. No files, snapshots"},{"u":"../help/repair.html","g":"User guide","t":"Repair","h":[["automatic-and-manual-repair","Automatic and manual repair"],["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Repair Repair collects the tools that can fix something. Each one diagnoses first. A repair is offered only when fresh evidence shows the exact problem it fixes, and it runs only after you turn on Auto-repair confirmed issues or confirm the repair in the inspector. Every repair is journaled and verified afterwards. Automatic and manual repair Auto-repair confirmed issues lets a diagnostic tool run the repairs it proves are needed after the scan. It is off by default. For a single repair, open the inspector with the report, choose the repair, read its preview, confirm it and press the repair button. See How changes stay safe for what happens before and after any change. Tools in this section Use this after a diagnosis identifies a problem. Repairs require confirmed evidence and explicit approval. Core Windows services Check Power, Plug and Play, User Manager, Group Policy Client, Network Store Interface, RPC Endpoint Mapper, Remote Procedure Call, DCOM Server Process Launcher, Background Tasks Infrastructure and CoreMessaging services together. Optional recovery starts only exact enabled automatic services that fresh evidence confirms are stopped after the startup window and complete dependency checks. Demand-start services remain unchanged when idle. Power plans, devices, drivers, accounts, credentials, profiles, policy, adapters, addresses, routes, DNS, firewall configuration, registrations, endpoints, task registrations, application data, service permissions and startup types are preserved. No policy refresh, device scan, account action, network change, power-setting change, registration change or task launch is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not end-to-end feature behavior. System files Inventory bounded Windows servicing package, optional-feature and capability states, check prerequisites, scan protected system files, and automatically choose SFC only when the live check confirms corruption. Pending package, feature or capability states request review but do not authorize installation, removal, cleanup, rollback, enablement or disablement. WMI repository Check WMI service availability, then repository consistency. Auto-repair can start an eligible stopped service and salvage confirmed corruption, then verify each result. Disabled service settings are preserved. Readable registrations are merged; unreadable content may require provider reinstallation. Management clients may be interrupted. No reset, deletion or automatic rollback. Windows system notifications Read recent Service Control Manager history, then check COM+ Event System and System Event Notification Service together. Optional recovery verifies COM+ Event System readiness before starting SENS. Existing logon, network, power and COM+ notifications may resume under current configuration. COM+ catalogs, subscriptions, notification registrations, consumers, accounts, permissions, recovery settings, policy and startup types are preserved. No subscription, registration, consumer, account, permission or policy is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not end-to-end notification delivery. Diagnostic Policy Service Review bounded Diagnostic Policy operational history, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical records cannot authorize service recovery or a troubleshooting action. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing diagnostic scenarios may resume. Policy, troubleshooting data and startup type are preserved. No troubleshooter or suggested fix is run. Verification covers stable service state only, not fault detection, diagnosis completion or repair outcome. Windows Error Reporting Review crash-dump configuration, bounded dump-file metadata, recent system crash reports, exact Windows Error Reporting service-failure history and current service readiness. Historical reports do not establish a current fault or root cause and cannot authorize recovery. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing queued reports may resume under current policy. Report archives, dumps, consent, upload policy and startup type are preserved. No upload or deletion is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not report creation, upload, delivery, problem resolution or crash diagnosis. Windows Event Collector Review bounded Windows Event Collector subscription inventory, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. An empty inventory can be intentional, and subscription count or historical failures cannot authorize recovery. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing subscriptions may resume remote event collection. Subscriptions, forwarded events, credentials, channel settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not source reachability, authentication, remote delivery, event freshness or subscription health. Windows Event Log Inspect four diagnostic channels for enabled state, retention mode, current size, configured limit, record count and saturation, then check Windows Event Log service availability. Auto-repair can start a stopped, enabled service after checking dependencies and verify running state. A separately confirmed focused action can enable one exact disabled unmanaged diagnostic channel while preserving every other reported setting. Logs, channel permissions, retention, size limits, startup types and managed settings are preserved. Successful event delivery and archive capacity remain unverified. Windows Time service Check Windows Time configuration, service and synchronization. Auto-repair can start W32Time when the current status reports the exact stopped-service error and fresh policy, mode and dependency checks pass, then request a resync and verify fresh status. Thi"},{"u":"../help/updates.html","g":"User guide","t":"Updates","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Updates Updates covers Windows Update, the services behind it and the component store. You can see what is installed, what is pending and why an update fails, and you can review available updates before choosing any to install. Tools in this section Watch servicing activity, review available updates and repair the Windows Update chain. Servicing activity monitor Observe bounded Windows Update and Setup servicing activity over one or more snapshots. Options control refresh timing and whether update events, update history, the exact Windows Update scheduled task, exact service states, restart indicators, current-account background transfers, Delivery Optimization activity and privacy-bounded Windows Installer activity are included. Process IDs, command lines, users and paths are excluded. Activity does not prove progress, completion, a stall or a fault. No update, setup, transfer, task, service, process, policy, restart or repair action is requested. Available updates Discover selected software and driver updates using the configured source, Windows Update or an already registered Microsoft Update service. Source readiness is checked before discovery. No installation, license acceptance, source registration or policy changes. This is separate from servicing repair. Windows Update Inventory bounded Windows servicing package, optional-feature and capability states, check restart indicators, free space and update services, observe Delivery Optimization without starting it, then scan the component store and protected system files. Pending package, feature or capability states request review but do not authorize a component action. Auto-repair can start enabled Windows Update, BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer and Update Orchestrator services. Starting Update Orchestrator may resume existing scheduled update activity under current policy. Packages, optional features, capabilities, Delivery Optimization cache, peer, bandwidth, policy, source enrollment, schedules and startup settings are preserved. DISM or SFC requires live evidence of corruption. No update is selected or approved by this workflow. Windows Update Service Check exact Windows Update Service state, its registered service DLL, bounded current update activity and exact service-failure history. DLL observations, update activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or update operation. Optional recovery starts only a stopped automatic service after fresh dependency and executable-registration checks. A stopped demand-start service remains informational. Existing update activity may resume under current policy. Update policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects, approves or installs no update. Verification covers stable service state only, not source reachability, applicability, download, installation, completion or restart readiness. Delivery Optimization Check the exact Delivery Optimization service state. A stopped demand-start service remains informational. Only an exact Paused state can offer recovery. Optional recovery repeats the state check, sends one bounded continue request and requires two coherent Running observations. Startup type, cache contents, peers, bandwidth, update source and managed policy are preserved. Transfer activity, cache health, update progress and completion remain unverified. BITS service Check the exact Background Intelligent Transfer Service state, current-account transfer jobs and bounded exact service-failure history. Historical failures and transfer snapshots cannot authorize a service start. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume queued background transfers. Jobs, priorities, owners, destinations, proxy settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not transfer progress, application ownership or completion. Background transfers (BITS) Read BITS transfer jobs in the selected account scope and review exact job identities. Queue names do not establish application ownership. No transfers are resumed, completed, canceled, deleted or reassigned. Cryptographic Services Check exact Cryptographic Services state, its registered service DLL and bounded exact service-failure history. Historical failures and DLL observations cannot authorize a service start or component replacement. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing catalog, certificate and update operations may resume. Certificates, keys, catalogs, trust settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not certificate, signature, catalog or trust validation. Windows Modules Installer Check exact Windows Modules Installer state, bounded servicing package transitions and exact service-failure history. Historical failures and package states cannot authorize a service start or package operation. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing servicing activity may resume. Packages, component-store content, sources, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not component-store health, package applicability, servicing completion or restart readiness. Windows Update Medic Service Check exact Windows Update Medic Service state, its registered service DLL, bounded current update activity and exact service-failure history. DLL observations, update activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or component action. A stopped demand-start service can be normal. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can start only the stopped enabled service after fresh dependency checks. Windows remediation may resume under current policy and can repair update "},{"u":"../help/devices.html","g":"User guide","t":"Devices","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Devices Devices covers printers, audio, cameras, Bluetooth, input and the Windows services that attached hardware relies on. Tools in this section Printing, audio, cameras, Bluetooth, input and the services behind attached hardware. Printing Check the Print Spooler, local queue pauses, visible print jobs, offline settings and driver-reported conditions, then inventory queue, driver, port, Standard TCP/IP endpoint, print processor and print monitor registrations. Correlate recent PrintService problems only when one exact current queue identity is present. Assigned Standard TCP/IP endpoints receive one bounded connection attempt without application data, SNMP or a print job. Auto-repair can start the enabled spooler, resume only exact paused queues with fresh waiting-job evidence, and clear offline mode. Idle intentional pauses remain review-only. Remote connections, SNMP community strings, extension registrations and job contents are preserved. A condition code or successful connection does not establish printer identity, supplies, mechanics, queue acceptance, rendering success or physical printing. Audio Check the default multimedia input and output identities and shared formats, audio device and driver state, Audio Endpoint Builder and Windows Audio. Auto-repair starts stopped, enabled services and verifies running state. Startup settings, drivers, selected devices, exclusive mode and volume levels are preserved. Sound playback, recording and application routing remain untested. Camera support Inspect camera and imaging device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Windows Camera Frame Server availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. An application already requesting camera access may resume under existing permissions. Camera privacy settings, permissions, device configuration, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No reset, permission change or driver installation. Verification covers reported device configuration and service running state only, not image capture, privacy access or physical operation. Bluetooth support Inspect Bluetooth device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Bluetooth Support Service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks; paired devices may reconnect. Pairings, radio settings, device configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. No reset, device removal or driver installation. Verification covers reported device configuration and service running state only, not discovery, pairing, audio or data transfer. Smart card support Inspect smart-card reader errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Smart Card service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Applications may resume detecting inserted cards under existing policy. Certificates, PINs, credentials, reader and card configuration, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No initialization, enrollment, PIN operation, reader reset or driver installation. Verification covers reported reader configuration and service running state only, not card detection, authentication or application access. Biometric support Inspect biometric device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Windows Biometric Service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Existing Windows Hello and application requests may resume under current policy. Biometric templates, Windows Hello data, credentials, device configuration, permissions, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No enrollment, template deletion, sensor reset, permission change or driver installation. Verification covers reported sensor configuration and service running state only, not capture, matching, sign-in or application access. Human Interface Device Service Check Human Interface Device Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing hot buttons, media controls and other HID features may resume. Devices, drivers, button mappings, accessibility settings, input settings, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, driver, mapping or input setting operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not button, control or application behavior. Portable Device Enumerator Service Check Portable Device Enumerator Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume detecting and accessing connected portable devices. Devices, drivers, files, libraries, synchronization settings, permissions, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, file, library, synchronization or transfer operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not enumeration, access, synchronization or transfer. Windows Image Acquisition Check Windows Image Acquisition service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing scanner and still-image acquisition requests may resume under current application permissions. Devices, drivers, image data, application permissions, privacy settings, transfer settings, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, driver, image, permission, transfer or acquisition operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not scanner, camera, transfer or application operation. Shell Hardware Detection Inspect bounded storage-controller, disk and portable-device Plug and Play evidence and installed driver references, then check Shell Hardware Detection service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing AutoPlay a"},{"u":"../help/network.html","g":"User guide","t":"Network","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Network Network inspects connections, DNS, routes, wireless and wired sign-in, file sharing and remote access. Tools that reach another host use the target and timeout you set, and nothing is changed unless you confirm a repair. Tools in this section Inspect connections, DNS, routes, wireless, sharing and remote access. Network Inspect adapters, address state and origin, gateways, DNS configuration, effective DNS namespace policy, TCP settings, native Winsock protocol and namespace-provider catalog structure, the IP routing table and Hosts file mappings. Auto-repair can start enabled DNS Client, DHCP Client, SMB client, Network Location Awareness, Network List and IP Helper services and renew connected physical DHCP adapters lacking a usable IPv4 address. Starting network-identification services may recalculate network identity and apply a different existing firewall profile. IP Helper may activate existing IPv6 transition, port-proxy and network-notification features. Service recovery verifies running state, not successful name resolution, route selection, network identification or tunnel operation. Routes, Hosts entries, DNS servers, profiles, firewall rules, Winsock providers, transition configuration, startup types and policies are preserved. Renewal may change leased addresses and interrupt connections. Static settings and virtual adapters are preserved. Exact gateway ICMP responses do not verify Internet reachability. Wireless networking Inspect network devices and WLAN AutoConfig availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks; saved profiles may reconnect automatically. Profiles, credentials, policy, radio settings and startup types are preserved. No adapter reset or driver installation. Verification covers running state, not wireless authentication or connectivity. Wired authentication (802.1X) Inspect network devices and Wired AutoConfig for networks requiring 802.1X. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Existing authentication profiles may activate and change network access. Certificates, credentials, profiles, policy and startup settings are preserved. Verification covers running state only, not authentication or connectivity. Network Connections Review bounded current network profile assignments, exact service-failure history and current Network Connections service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a network change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing network-management interfaces may resume. Adapters, addresses, routes, DNS settings, connection profiles, credentials, firewall configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. No connection or network setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not adapter operation, address assignment, routing, name resolution or connectivity. WinHTTP Auto-Proxy Review stored WinHTTP defaults, current-account Internet settings, exact service-failure history and current WinHTTP Auto-Proxy readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Proxy configuration and historical failures cannot authorize service recovery or discovery. A stopped demand-start service can be normal. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can start only the stopped enabled service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume WPAD or PAC discovery under current settings. Proxy definitions, PAC URLs, credentials, network settings, firewall configuration, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. No URL is fetched and no proxy or network setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not discovery, reachability, authentication, policy ownership or application use. Windows Connection Manager Review bounded current network profile assignments, exact service-failure history and current Windows Connection Manager readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a connection decision. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Windows may resume automatic connection decisions under existing profiles and policy. Adapters, profiles, credentials, metering, cost, roaming, firewall, VPN and authentication settings and startup type are preserved. No profile, adapter or setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not profile applicability, connection selection, authentication, routing, name resolution or connectivity. Network Setup Service Review bounded current adapter protocol bindings, exact service-failure history and current Network Setup Service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Binding state and historical failures cannot authorize service recovery or a setup change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing pending network setup may resume under Windows policy. Adapters, drivers, driver packages, bindings, addresses, routes, DNS settings, profiles, credentials, firewall configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no driver or configuration change. Verification covers stable service state only, not adapter availability, driver suitability, setup completion, address assignment, routing, name resolution or connectivity. File sharing (SMB) Check Windows SMB server availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped Server service after fresh dependency checks. Existing file and printer shares may become reachable under their current permissions and firewall policy. No share, permission, SMB setting, firewall rule, credential, policy or startup type is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not client access or file transfer. Remot"},{"u":"../help/apps.html","g":"User guide","t":"Apps","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Apps Apps reviews installed programs, Store packages, Office and Outlook, browser extensions, startup apps and search. Removal and reset tools always show a review first and change only what you select. Tools in this section Review installed applications, Store packages, Office and Outlook, browser extensions and search. Installed applications Read application uninstall registrations in the selected machine or running-account scope, then combine that scope with bounded Application error, Reliability Monitor and restart evidence. Other accounts and portable apps are excluded. Registrations and broad history are not exact application-health or ownership proof. No installer, uninstaller, application, process, service, file, registration, account, policy or restart action is selected. App packages Inventory registered Appx/MSIX packages for the selected account scope. Running account is the default; all users requires administrator access. Report exact identities, framework and non-removable flags, and dependency counts. Provisioned packages, Win32 applications, managed ownership and application data are not evaluated. No packages are removed. Browser extensions Inventory bounded Chrome, Edge, Brave and Firefox extension identities from the running account. Browser history, credentials, extension settings, manifest contents, commands and policy values are excluded. Presence does not prove enablement, trust, ownership or safe removal. No browser, extension or policy is changed. Startup applications Inventory machine and running-account Run and RunOnce registration names, Startup folder file names, Windows startup approval states, and boot or logon scheduled-task names and states, then return one startup-environment conclusion. Command lines, target paths, binary approval timestamps, task actions, arguments, accounts and credentials are not collected. Registration does not prove an item ran, caused a delay, is unwanted or is safe to disable. Approval state does not prove a matching registration exists. No registration, file, application, task, service or policy change is selected. Microsoft app removal Review selected OneDrive, Outlook, Office/M365, Project and Teams registrations with account-scope and retention choices. Machine, running-account and registered-package inventories are correlated; all-user review also includes packages provisioned for new users. A supported current-account package can receive a separate exact safeguard plan in the desktop or CLI. Native removal remains disconnected. No process, application, package, file, credential, folder location, registry value or user data is changed. Outlook reset Review New Outlook, Classic Outlook or both with explicit choices for caches, profile settings, authentication, OST files, add-ins and New Outlook reinstall. Running-account review checks bounded known locations, exact registrations and closed-process readiness. A separately confirmed cache-only action retains exact cache directories under unique recovery names and verifies every move. All-user review and other reset areas remain review-only. No retained cache, profile, OST file, credential, add-in, package or setting is removed. Foxit inventory Inventory machine-installed Foxit products and updater registry values in both views. User installations, effective policy and product ownership are not verified. No applications or settings are changed. Windows Installer service Check Installer restriction, rollback, elevation, logging and debug policy, in-progress markers, engine binaries and registered cached-package visibility before service recovery. Correlate recent failed and successful outcomes only for exact reported product identities. If prerequisites pass, auto-repair can start the enabled service and verify Running. Historical outcomes, logging, cache visibility and debug findings do not authorize package or policy changes. Policies, logs, transaction markers, startup types and cached packages are preserved. A specific package installation is not tested. Windows Search Check Windows Search service availability, current SystemIndex activity and recent Search event history. Auto-repair can start a stopped, enabled service and verify running state. A separate explicit index-rebuild choice retains the prior index, starts a fresh index and verifies exact service and directory state. Indexed locations, policy, startup type and retained data are preserved. Starting the service or rebuilding the index can resume background indexing. Search result completeness remains untested. App Readiness Check App Readiness service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing first-sign-in and application provisioning may resume under current policy. Packages, registrations, licenses, user profiles, application data, policy and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no package operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not sign-in, provisioning or application launch. State Repository Service Check State Repository Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing application-model state requests may resume. Registrations, packages, licenses, profiles, browser and application data, repository content, policy and startup type are preserved. No reset, re-registration or deletion is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not repository consistency or application operation. AppX Deployment Service Check AppX Deployment Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing deployment, registration or servicing requests may resume under current policy. Packages, registrations, licenses, profiles, application data, repository content, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no package operation. Verific"},{"u":"../help/maintenance.html","g":"User guide","t":"Maintenance","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Maintenance Maintenance keeps a machine tidy: file cleanup, old user profiles, scheduled tasks and decluttering Windows. Every tool previews what it would remove before anything is deleted. Tools in this section Clean up files, profiles and scheduled tasks, and declutter Windows. Cleanup Estimate selected temporary-file, cache and retained diagnostic-evidence targets using the age and file-limit settings in the main panel. Windows Temp and Windows SystemTemp are independent targets. Delivery Optimization is a separate cache choice and removed content may need to be downloaded again. Optional permanent removal uses the separate cleanup checkbox, rechecks retained candidates within the selected limit and records each outcome. Deletion bypasses the Recycle Bin. Targets, age and file limits are also configurable through the cleanup CLI command. Profiles Review Windows profiles using the selected age threshold, exact ProfileList registrations and optional service and scheduled-task account dependencies, then return one bounded review conclusion. Loaded, system and current-account profiles are excluded from review eligibility. Candidates remain review-only. No profile, folder, registry key, account, service, task, credential, policy or file is changed, and no removal is selected. Application dependencies and backup recoverability remain unverified. Scheduled tasks Inventory visible scheduled tasks, states and reported principals. No tasks are started, registered or changed. Actions, triggers and execution success are not verified. Declutter Windows Review explicit Windows cleanup areas such as Recall and AI, telemetry, search suggestions, consumer content, taskbar and Start, notifications, Edge, power, hibernation, Office languages and preinstalled software for the running account or all profiles. Review is read-only. High performance power, sleep and display timers, and hibernation can each be changed when that area is the only selection, with fresh evidence and explicit confirmation."},{"u":"../help/onboarding.html","g":"User guide","t":"Onboarding","h":[["checking-an-installer-before-you-run-it","Checking an installer before you run it"],["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Onboarding Onboarding prepares a new computer. It reviews the applications and configuration you select and names the computer. It does not download or install anything by itself. Checking an installer before you run it The command line can verify a vendor installer before you run it: KillerBench trusts an installer only when it carries a valid Authenticode signature from the vendor's exact organization name. It never runs the installer. Tools in this section Prepare a new computer: applications, name and configuration. Onboarding Review explicitly selected provisioning applications and configuration groups. Current uninstall, app-package and Office Click-to-Run registrations are correlated without downloading or executing installers. Configuration selections describe consequences and route to separately guarded workflows when implemented. No download, installation, account, update, cleanup, policy, registry, service, profile or restart action occurs. Computer name Review the proposed computer name against current identity and domain role. No rename, credential collection or restart. Name availability, permissions and application dependencies remain unverified."},{"u":"../help/security.html","g":"User guide","t":"Security","h":[["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Security Security inspects Microsoft Defender, the firewall, certificates and the sign-in services. It also exposes the TPM and platform security state that BitLocker and Windows Hello depend on. Tools in this section Inspect protection, firewall, certificates and sign-in services. Microsoft Defender Inspect Defender protection and signature status. Auto-repair refreshes signatures only when Defender reports active protection and outdated signatures. Uses configured signature sources. No protection, exclusion or policy changes; no malware scan. Windows Security Center Review bounded antivirus registrations with raw provider state, exact service-failure history and current Windows Security Center readiness without interpreting protection health. An empty inventory is not a protection-health verdict, and registration count, raw state or historical failures cannot authorize recovery or a security change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing security status monitoring and notifications may resume. Antivirus configuration, provider registrations, protection settings, exclusions, notifications, policy and startup type are preserved. No scan, update or protection change is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not antivirus presence, protection state, signature freshness, notification delivery, registration ownership or effective policy. Firewall services Read Domain, Private and Public profile enabled settings from ActiveStore, then check Base Filtering Engine and Windows Defender Firewall services. Optional recovery starts only enabled, stopped services after fresh dependency checks. Existing filtering policy may take effect and interrupt network or remote access. Startup types, service permissions, firewall rules, profiles and IPsec settings are preserved. No resets, rule changes or service stops. Verification establishes service running state only; effective protection and connectivity remain unverified. Certificate expiration Inspect certificate dates and build cache-only local trust chains for the machine and current-user personal stores, then return one bounded operational conclusion. Revocation, server presentation, bindings, enrollment, policy ownership, remote trust and private-key usability remain unverified. No certificate, trust setting, enrollment, binding, private key, application, service or policy is changed. Credential Manager Check Credential Manager service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume requests for stored credentials. Vault contents, passwords, passkeys, certificates, tokens, credential registrations, permissions, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. No credential content or operation is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not credential retrieval, authentication or application access. Web Account Manager Review payload-sanitized account broker history and exact Web Account Manager availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume account and token requests under current policy. Historical events cannot authorize recovery or establish current account state. Accounts, credentials, tokens, cookies, application registrations, permissions, sign-in state, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no account, credential, token, cookie, permission or sign-in operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not authentication, token issuance or application access. Microsoft Account Sign-in Assistant Review payload-sanitized account broker history and exact Microsoft Account Sign-in Assistant availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing Microsoft account sign-in requests may resume under current policy. Historical events cannot authorize recovery or establish current account state. Accounts, credentials, tokens, device registration, application data, permissions, sign-in state, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no account, credential, token, registration, permission or sign-in operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not authentication, account connectivity or application access."},{"u":"../help/bitlocker.html","g":"User guide","t":"BitLocker","h":[["using-the-bitlocker-tool","Using the BitLocker tool"],["more-bitlocker-actions","More BitLocker actions"],["tpm-and-fips","TPM and FIPS"],["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"BitLocker BitLocker inspects drive encryption, protectors and recovery information, then helps you back up, protect or change a drive. It works on this computer only. Using the BitLocker tool Choose the drive letter and an Operation , then press GO . - Inspect reads encryption state, protection status and protector identities. It is the default and changes nothing. - BackupRecovery writes the selected recovery password to a new file outside the protected drive and verifies it. Press Find recovery protectors first, choose the protector, then confirm the backup. - Encrypt backs up the selected recovery password, verifies the backup, then starts encryption once with the method you choose. The Windows drive needs a TPM protector and a recovery password. A data drive needs only a recovery password. - ResumeProtection turns protection back on for a fully encrypted drive where it was suspended. Each of these needs its own confirmation box, and KillerBench rechecks the drive immediately before it acts. More BitLocker actions Open More BitLocker actions for changes that sit outside GO. Choose an action, press Preview to read exactly what it requires, then confirm and press Run action . - Turn on auto-unlock makes an encrypted data drive unlock when Windows starts. The Windows drive must be fully encrypted with protection on. - Add a TPM protector or Add a recovery password creates one new protector. KillerBench never shows or logs a new recovery password, so back it up straight away. - Back up the recovery password to Active Directory or Microsoft Entra ID sends the protector you selected above. KillerBench reports that Windows accepted the request. It cannot read the directory copy, so confirm it there. - Decrypt this drive starts decryption, for example before encrypting again with a required method. It needs a second confirmation because the drive is unprotected afterwards, and it refuses the Windows drive while other drives auto-unlock from it. TPM and FIPS If the TPM is present but not ready, the Security section can prepare it without clearing it. KillerBench never clears a TPM, because that destroys keys held by BitLocker and Windows Hello. The FIPS algorithm policy switch is available as an explicit, reversible repair for organizations that need FIPS-approved BitLocker PINs. Tools in this section Inspect encryption, protectors and recovery information, then back them up or protect the drive. BitLocker Preview BitLocker options and inspect the selected volume. No encryption or recovery backup is performed by this workflow."},{"u":"../help/privacy.html","g":"User guide","t":"Privacy","h":[["reviewing-and-changing-settings","Reviewing and changing settings"],["tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"]],"x":"Privacy Privacy reads the privacy settings for this machine and the account running KillerBench, then lets you change the ones you choose. It goes beyond the Windows Settings app, covering app permissions, cloud search and search highlights. Reviewing and changing settings Select the privacy areas to inspect and press GO . Every area starts unchecked and the review is read-only. To change a setting, use the Privacy setting control. KillerBench checks whether a policy or device management already owns the setting, records the current value, makes the one change you asked for and reads it back to confirm it. Settings owned by policy are left alone. The settings include diagnostic data and advertising choices, clipboard history, 17 app-permission settings such as camera, microphone and location, cloud content search for personal and work accounts, and search highlights. Tools in this section Review and change app permissions, suggestions, search and advertising settings. Privacy Read selected privacy registry settings for this machine and the account running KillerBench. Separate explicit controls can change Clipboard history or a supported account setting after fresh policy checks, durable preparation and exact verification. Other privacy selections remain read-only. Registry snapshots do not prove effective permissions or policy ownership."},{"u":"../help/reports.html","g":"User guide","t":"Reports and evidence","h":[["states-you-will-see","States you will see"],["raw-output-and-the-readable-report","Raw output and the readable report"],["saved-logs","Saved logs"],["exporting","Exporting"]],"x":"Reports and evidence Every run produces a report you can read, filter and export. The report always separates what was proven from what was only observed. States you will see State Meaning --- --- Needs review The evidence supports a problem that deserves attention. Information Evidence was collected and is shown for context. Incomplete or Unknown A check could not finish or the evidence was unavailable. It is never counted as healthy. Historical An event from the past. It explains history but does not prove a current fault. Verified A repair or change was confirmed by reading the result back. Raw output and the readable report Raw output keeps the technical evidence with original line numbers so you can trace any finding back to its source. The Readable report summarizes the same run in plain language, with the next safe step first. Saved logs KillerBench can also read saved Windows logs, such as CBS and Setup logs, without running anything on the machine. Findings link to the exact line that produced them. Load sample in Session sources opens a synthetic example you can explore safely. Exporting Export report saves the report with its source paths, parsed entries and evidence excerpts, ready to attach to a ticket. Recovery passwords, credentials and other secrets are never included."},{"u":"../help/cli.html","g":"User guide","t":"Command line","h":[["updates-and-servicing","Updates and servicing"],["bitlocker-and-tpm","BitLocker and TPM"],["security-and-privacy","Security and privacy"],["services","Services"],["storage-files-and-backup","Storage, files and backup"],["printing","Printing"],["network","Network"],["apps","Apps"],["system","System"],["knowledge-validation-and-engine","Knowledge, validation and engine"],["other-commands","Other commands"]],"x":"Command line killerbench-cli runs the same Engine as the desktop app from PowerShell or a remote console. Every command that could change the machine previews by default and needs an explicit flag such as --execute before it acts. Commands print JSON evidence to standard output and progress to standard error. Exit code 0 means the preview or verified result succeeded, 3 means blocked, failed or unverified, 2 means the arguments were not recognized, and 130 means you stopped it. Run killerbench-cli with no arguments to print this list on your own machine. Updates and servicing - bits-job-review JOB_GUID --all-users --observe : Exact transfer review or two-snapshot observation; no retry. - bits-jobs --all-users : BITS job states, owners and byte counts; no transfer changes. - bits-retry JOB_GUID --execute : Preview by default; explicit exact-job retry, no application-completion claim. - bits-retry-recover JOB_GUID : Two read-only snapshots; archives stable outcomes without another retry. - component-repair-source : Managed DISM repair-source policy and conflicts; no changes. - delivery-optimization : Read-only Delivery Optimization service state; JSON evidence. - delivery-optimization-activity : Privacy-bounded current job counts and byte totals; no transfer identities or changes. - delivery-optimization-policy : Read-only classic and Policy CSP settings; sensitive values and provider identities excluded. - feature-service-events : Read exact Time, Search, WMI, Event Log and audio service failure history. - feature-update-policy : Read-only target release, deferral, pause and safeguard policy; JSON evidence. - pending-file-operations : Read-only privacy-bounded pending file operation summary; JSON evidence. - restart-check : Read-only pending restart sources; JSON evidence. - servicing-logs : Bounded newest CBS, plain-text and compressed CBS archives, DISM and Windows Setup log analysis; no repair. - servicing-monitor --refresh 1..60 --samples 1..120 --skip-events --skip-history --skip-services --skip-restart --skip-setup --skip-compatibility --skip-readiness --skip-transfers --skip-delivery-optimization --skip-installer --skip-scheduled-task : Read-only Servicing activity snapshots. - servicing-service-events : Read-only exact Windows Update servicing-chain failure histories. - servicing-service-states : Read-only current state for the six-service Windows Update servicing chain. - setup-code DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Documented Windows Setup result-code reference; no machine inspection or repair. - update-applicability : Read-only Windows edition, build and architecture evidence. - update-cache : Bounded read-only SoftwareDistribution cache totals; no filenames. - update-code DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Documented Windows Update result-code reference; no machine inspection or repair. - update-context : Read-only current elevation and desktop-session evidence; no account identifiers. - update-engine-files : Read-only native Windows Update engine presence, version and signature evidence. - update-events : Read-only Windows Update Client history from the last seven days; JSON including raw evidence. - update-followup DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Fresh read-only diagnostics selected by a documented Windows Update result code. - update-history : Read-only newest 100 Windows Update operation outcomes; JSON evidence. - update-plan --select GUID:REVISION --drivers --no-software --allow-restart-required --json --execute : Repeat --select; preview by default. - update-policy : Read-only managed Windows Update policy; JSON evidence. - update-policy-surfaces : Read-only classic and Policy CSP configuration-surface evidence; values and provider identities excluded. - update-recover : Check a pending update; never installs or retries it. - update-restart-policy : Read-only compliance deadlines, grace periods, restart suppression and notification behavior. - update-search --drivers --no-software --filter TITLE --downloaded-only --classification GUID --product GUID : JSON discovery and matches; no installation. - update-service-events : Read-only exact Windows Update service failure history. - update-service-files : Read-only exact Windows Update service DLL registration and signature evidence. - update-services : Read-only Windows Update service registrations; JSON evidence. - update-store-reset --source Configured WindowsUpdate MicrosoftUpdate --drivers --no-software --execute : Fresh-search gated; preview by default; retains the old store. - update-store-reset-recover : Reconcile a prepared store reset without repeating it. - windows-capabilities : Read-only online Windows capability states; JSON result. - windows-update-task : Read-only exact Scheduled Start task state and runtime history; no task execution or update operation. BitLocker and TPM - bitlocker-auto-unlock DRIVE: --execute : Preview by default; turns on auto-unlock for an encrypted data volume. - bitlocker-auto-unlock-recover : Verify an interrupted auto-unlock request without repeating it. - bitlocker-backup DRIVE: PROTECTOR_GUID FILE_PATH --execute : Preview by default; new recovery backup file only. - bitlocker-backup-recover DRIVE: PROTECTOR_GUID FILE_PATH : Verify an interrupted backup without writing it again. - bitlocker-decrypt DRIVE: --execute --confirm-unprotected : Preview by default; starts decryption, for example before re-encrypting with a required method. - bitlocker-decrypt-recover : Verify an interrupted decryption request without repeating it. - bitlocker-encrypt DRIVE: RECOVERY_PROTECTOR_GUID NEW_BACKUP_FILE --encryption XtsAes128 --used-space-only --execute : Preview by default; requires existing TPM and recovery protectors. - bitlocker-encrypt-recover : Verify pending conversion state without repeating the request. - bitlocker-escrow DRIVE: RECOVERY_PROTECTOR_GUID ad entra --execute : Preview by default; backs up an existing recovery password to Active Directory or Microsoft Entra ID. - bitlocker-plan --volume C: --operation Encrypt --encrypt"},{"u":"../help/troubleshooting.html","g":"User guide","t":"Troubleshooting","h":[["an-action-says-it-is-blocked","An action says it is blocked"],["a-previous-request-needs-reconciliation","A previous request needs reconciliation"],["a-check-shows-incomplete-or-unknown","A check shows Incomplete or Unknown"],["where-files-are-kept","Where files are kept"],["reporting-a-problem","Reporting a problem"]],"x":"Troubleshooting An action says it is blocked A blocked result means a safety check failed, and nothing was changed. The message names the reason. The most common reasons are below. - Administrator access is required. Close KillerBench and start it again with Run as administrator . - Restart readiness must be clear. Windows has a restart pending. Restart the computer, then run the action again. - Managed policy is present. Group policy or device management owns the setting. Change it there instead. - Evidence changed. Something changed between the first check and the final recheck. Run the tool again to read the new state. A previous request needs reconciliation KillerBench found a pending record from an interrupted request. Run the same action again. It checks what happened and reports the real outcome without repeating the request. On the command line, use the matching -recover command, for example killerbench-cli bitlocker-auto-unlock-recover . A check shows Incomplete or Unknown The evidence could not be read, often because KillerBench was not running as administrator or a Windows service was unavailable. KillerBench reports this honestly instead of assuming the computer is healthy. Where files are kept Journals, pending records and saved evidence live under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench . Each guarded action keeps its own folder. Keep them when you need a record of what changed. Reporting a problem KillerBench is in development. Include the exported report and the activity log when you report a problem, and remove anything you would not share."},{"u":"../engine/getting-started.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Getting started","h":[["requirements","Requirements"],["reference-the-project","Reference the project"],["run-a-focused-workflow","Run a focused workflow"],["run-the-broad-diagnostic-scan","Run the broad diagnostic scan"],["handle-cancellation","Handle cancellation"],["next-steps","Next steps"]],"x":"Getting started Run a read-only diagnostic workflow and inspect its typed result in a few minutes. Requirements - Windows 10 or Windows 11 - .NET 10 SDK - A .NET 10 application - A writable folder for workflow journals The Engine has no WPF dependency. The desktop interface is a separate host. Reference the project Add the Engine project to your solution, then reference it from your application: Run a focused workflow autoRepair: false keeps the run diagnostic-only. The Engine writes a workflow journal even when no repair is attempted. Run the broad diagnostic scan The full scan combines common diagnostic areas. It does not prove every possible Windows component healthy, and it does not enable every optional repair. Handle cancellation Pass a CancellationToken from your host. The Engine checks cancellation between bounded stages. If a repair may already have reached Windows, the durable journal remains available for reconciliation instead of silently retrying the action. Next steps - Read Diagnostic workflows before selecting workflow kinds. - Read Repairs and safety before exposing any repair action. - Use the CLI guide to explore diagnostics without writing a host application."},{"u":"../engine/workflows.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Diagnostic workflows","h":[["windows-update-medic-service","Windows Update Medic Service"],["focused-workflows","Focused workflows"],["check-states","Check states"],["full-system-scan","Full system scan"],["live-servicing-activity","Live servicing activity"],["progress-and-status","Progress and status"],["workflow-journals","Workflow journals"]],"x":"Diagnostic workflows Workflows collect related evidence and return one WorkflowResult . They are the simplest high-level Engine surface for desktop applications, automation hosts, and support tools. Windows Update Medic Service The focused update-medic-service workflow combines exact current WaaSMedicSvc state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize a start or component action. A stopped demand-start state remains informational in every broader workflow. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can issue one bounded start only after complete dependency, executable registration, administrator, restart and fresh final evidence checks pass. Starting the service can resume Windows remediation or update activity under the current configuration. KillerBench preserves update policy, source enrollment, schedules, packages, BITS jobs, restart settings, service permissions and startup type. Verification covers stable service state only. It does not claim remediation, component health, applicability, download, installation or restart readiness. The focused windows-update-service workflow combines exact current state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize its separately guarded start. The service can start only from a stopped automatic state after fresh dependency and executable-registration checks. Starting it may resume existing update activity under current policy. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish source reachability, applicability, download, installation, completion or restart readiness. BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer and Update Orchestrator use their own focused evidence combinations and conservative conclusions. Historical events remain read-only context and cannot authorize a start or component change. The focused delivery-optimization-service workflow shows exact service state, privacy-bounded current transfer aggregates, recognized policy surfaces, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Missing evidence takes precedence, while service, policy or historical concerns remain review-only. Historical failures cannot authorize continuation. Running and stopped demand-start states remain informational. Only an exact Paused current state can offer the separate recovery. Execution repeats that state immediately before one bounded continue request and requires two coherent Running observations. Startup type, cache contents, peers, bandwidth, update source and managed policy remain unchanged. Verification does not claim transfer activity, cache health, peer reachability, update progress or completion. The focused bits-service workflow shows current-account transfer jobs, exact current service state, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion before its service recipe is evaluated. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and transfer snapshots cannot authorize a start. Optional recovery still requires exact current stopped-service evidence, dependencies, executable registration, administrator access, clear restart evidence and one unchanged final check. Jobs, priorities, owners, destinations, proxy settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not prove transfer progress, application ownership, acknowledgment or completion. The focused cryptographic-service workflow shows exact current service state, registered service DLL presence and signature status, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and DLL observations cannot authorize a service start or component replacement. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Certificates, keys, catalogs, trust settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state and a present Valid DLL do not validate certificates, signatures, catalogs, trust chains, update payloads or application behavior. The focused modules-installer-service workflow shows exact current service state, bounded servicing package transitions, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and package states cannot authorize a service start or package operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Packages, component-store content, sources, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish component-store health, package applicability, servicing completion or restart readiness. The focused update-orchestrator-service workflow shows exact current service state, the exact built-in Windows Update Scheduled Start task, bounded current Windows Update activity, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Task results, activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or update operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish applicability, download progress, installation progress, "},{"u":"../engine/evidence.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Evidence and results","h":[["provider-evidence","Provider evidence"],["workflow-checks","Workflow checks"],["workflow-results","Workflow results"],["reports","Reports"],["historical-evidence","Historical evidence"],["unknown-is-a-result","Unknown is a result"]],"x":"Evidence and results The Engine separates provider output, interpreted checks, workflow results, and human-readable reports. Each layer keeps uncertainty visible. Provider evidence Native commands and Windows providers run behind fixed time, record, and size bounds. ToolResult records exit status, timeout, truncation, standard output, and error output. A provider failure or truncated result cannot be treated as a clean empty response. Collectors minimize retained data. Many diagnostic results keep names, categories, counts, states, and timestamps while excluding credentials, command lines, full paths, account identities, network endpoints, and unrelated event payloads. Workflow checks WorkflowCheck adds a name, interpreted state, explanation, optional repair kind, and optional provider evidence. Consumers should display the state and detail together. The state alone does not communicate the scope or limitations of the check. Workflow results WorkflowResult contains: - The workflow kind and machine - Start time and final status - Every completed check - Any repair results - Recipe results - The saved journal path Use the typed result for UI decisions. Use reports for people and JSON for interoperable automation. Reports KillerBench can generate diagnostic-only or diagnostic-and-repair reports. The report separates: 1. What was inspected 2. What problem was found 3. What evidence supports the finding 4. What action was proposed or performed 5. How the result was verified 6. What remains unknown or needs a restart HTML reports use the Killer Tools theme system and remain readable without the desktop app. Text reports favor portability. JSON retains structured fields for another program. Historical evidence Event records and imported logs describe what was recorded at a past time. They can identify useful patterns, error codes, and the next diagnostic scope. They do not prove that the fault is still active. Unknown is a result Unknown is not an exception to hide. It tells the host that access was denied, a source was missing, a provider failed, output was malformed, the bound was exceeded, or evidence disagreed. A useful host shows this limitation and offers the relevant next step."},{"u":"../engine/repairs.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Repairs and safety","h":[["repair-sequence","Repair sequence"],["explicit-choices","Explicit choices"],["managed-systems","Managed systems"],["durable-pending-records","Durable pending records"],["automatic-repair","Automatic repair"],["host-responsibility","Host responsibility"]],"x":"Repairs and safety KillerBench repairs are typed operations with eligibility, execution, verification, and recovery rules. They are not arbitrary command strings selected from a log line. Repair sequence 1. Collect complete current evidence. 2. Match one supported recipe. 3. Review required options and consequences. 4. Confirm administrative and policy prerequisites. 5. Recollect the decisive evidence immediately before mutation. 6. Write a durable preparation record. 7. Run one bounded native action. 8. Collect fresh verification evidence. 9. Record a verified, blocked, stopped, or uncertain outcome. If the decisive evidence changes before execution, the Engine stops without performing the action. Explicit choices Option-dependent repairs require exact choices from the host. Examples include a selected volume, printer queue, recovery-key destination, power values, package version, or repair source. The generic automatic runner cannot invent these values. Managed systems Group Policy, device management, and other authority evidence can make a local repair ineffective or inappropriate. Supported coordinators inspect applicable authority state and fail closed when ownership is unknown or managed. Durable pending records A process can stop after Windows receives a request but before verification completes. In that case the Engine retains a pending record. A later reconciliation reads fresh state and determines what happened. Reconciliation never repeats the original mutation automatically. Automatic repair autoRepair means the host allows eligible recipes to proceed. It does not bypass evidence requirements, choose missing options, ignore policy, retry an uncertain action, restart Windows, or turn an Attention finding into a repair. The full system scan remains diagnostic-only even when a focused workflow allows automatic repair. Saved captures, imported evidence, and historical events do not authorize native dispatch. Host responsibility Before enabling a repair, a host should show: - The exact target - The proposed change - Required elevation or restart - Data and settings that remain untouched - The evidence that made the action eligible - The journal and recovery state after execution Use disposable virtual machines to validate native mutations across Windows versions and failure states before exposing them to production users."},{"u":"../engine/cli.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"CLI guide","h":[["build","Build"],["discover-commands","Discover commands"],["discover-workflow-controls","Discover workflow controls"],["run-a-workflow","Run a workflow"],["run-focused-diagnostics","Run focused diagnostics"],["live-servicing-monitor","Live servicing monitor"],["review-microsoft-app-removal-package-eligibility","Review Microsoft app removal package eligibility"],["export-the-vm-validation-plan","Export the VM validation plan"],["execution-boundary","Execution boundary"],["exit-behavior","Exit behavior"]],"x":"CLI guide killerbench-cli exposes the Engine for technicians, scripts, test harnesses, and headless collection. Build Discover commands The help output is the authoritative command list for the current checkout. Commands include focused diagnostics, complete workflows, servicing activity, knowledge lookups, planning tools, report generation, and selected guarded repairs. Discover workflow controls The command returns host-neutral JSON for the option-rich workflows. It includes toggles, sliders, choices, text fields, defaults, bounds, dependencies and the execution boundary. Repeated --set KEY=VALUE arguments validate and normalize a proposed option plan, fill omitted defaults and identify an explicit machine-changing request. Validation does not execute the plan, inspect Windows or bypass the separate execution coordinator. Run a workflow Add --json when a command supports structured output: Validated shared controls can run the matching read-only workflow directly: The workflow command accepts repeated --set KEY=VALUE values only after the shared validator accepts the complete plan. It refuses --auto-repair , recipe selection and every option marked as machine-changing. Use the dedicated preview and guarded execution command for a requested repair. The first diagnostic result records the nondefault settings in effect. Sensitive paths, installer switches, protector IDs, package sources and deployment device names are redacted or counted. A prepared System Restore description and reason drive its creation-readiness review. Storage repair review collects exact-volume state plus a bounded read-only filesystem diagnosis, then explains backup, restart or dismount requirements without scheduling CHKDSK. A selected storage optimization method drives the exact-volume analysis. Printing selections correlate one exact queue with current condition and attribute evidence, and one exact job with the bounded visible-job inventory. A selected MSI product is reviewed against its exact registration state, current Installer activity, restart state and administrator access. A selected Windows Time source is reviewed against current role, policy, mode and peer evidence. A selected Event Log channel receives an exact configuration and policy-ownership review. Selected Firewall profiles receive an exact ActiveStore and policy-ownership review. A proposed computer name receives an exact identity, role, restart and administrator readiness review. None of these reviews performs or authorizes the proposed change. Run focused diagnostics Focused commands are useful when a host needs one evidence boundary instead of a full workflow. Examples include: Live servicing monitor The monitor prints completed snapshots as they arrive. It reports recognized activity and evidence, not an invented completion percentage. Review Microsoft app removal package eligibility The plan command repeats current Microsoft app removal inventory and safeguard collection, then evaluates one exact package full name. It is preview-only and connects no removal provider. Preserved-data eligibility is limited to loose-file development packages because Windows does not offer that safeguard for ordinary installed Store/MSIX packages. An eligible result does not remove the package. Export the VM validation plan The command returns deterministic JSON scenario requirements for the installed recipe catalog. It runs no diagnostics or repairs. The output is a disposable-VM test plan, not evidence that a native scenario passed. Validate records returned from disposable VMs and summarize current-version coverage: Records must match the installed recipe version and scenario ID and contain every required artifact label and SHA-256 digest. Coverage keeps Passed, Failed, Blocked, Inconclusive and missing scenarios separate. These commands read the record files only. Generate a readable local coverage report from the same validated records: HTML reports include every installed scenario, status and text filters, and the 13 Killer Tools themes. Missing evidence remains distinct from every recorded outcome. Use --theme , --accent and --overwrite when needed. Report generation does not inspect or change Windows. Execution boundary Most commands are read-only. Commands that can change Windows require their exact execution switch and still enforce the same Engine eligibility, journaling, final-evidence, and verification rules. Unsupported execution is rejected. Exit behavior Treat JSON fields and documented exit behavior as the automation contract. Do not parse decorative console text when structured output is available. Preserve the journal path for later review or reconciliation."},{"u":"../engine/api.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"API guide","h":[["high-level-entry-points","High-level entry points"],["core-result-types","Core result types"],["workflow-controls","Workflow controls"],["capability-folders","Capability folders"],["cancellation-and-progress","Cancellation and progress"],["lower-level-collectors","Lower-level collectors"],["versioning","Versioning"]],"x":"API guide The Engine uses one public namespace, KillerBench.Engine , while source folders organize the implementation by capability. High-level entry points Task Start with --- --- Run one diagnostic area Workflows.RunAsync and WorkflowKind Run common diagnostics FullSystemScan.RunAsync Show available common areas CommonDiagnosticCatalog.All Show repair recipes RecipeCatalog.ForWorkflow Build workflow controls WorkflowOptionCatalog.All or WorkflowOptionCatalog.ForWorkflow Observe servicing activity ServicingActivityMonitor.ObserveAsync Interpret a completed workflow WorkflowResult and WorkflowRecoveryPlan.From Review a saved journal WorkflowJournalReview Generate reports Engine report and export types Core result types Type Purpose --- --- WorkflowCheck One interpreted diagnostic result with state, detail, evidence, and optional repair WorkflowResult Complete workflow output plus journal location ToolResult Bounded native-provider result including timeout and truncation state RepairRecipe Eligibility and execution metadata for one supported repair RecipeResult Outcome of evaluating or executing a recipe RepairResult Native repair steps, verification, and durable outcome WorkflowOptionSet Host-neutral controls, defaults and execution boundary for one option-rich workflow WorkflowOptionDefinition A toggle, slider, choice or text control with validation metadata and dependencies WorkflowOptionValidationResult Normalized values, validation errors and whether an explicit machine-changing control was requested WorkflowOptionProjection Converts a valid read-only option plan into Engine workflow or servicing-monitor options Workflow controls WorkflowOptionCatalog lets a desktop, CLI or embedded host discover the same supported controls without copying UI definitions. Each option has a stable key, label, control type, default value and explanation. Sliders include their minimum, maximum and step. Choices expose exact values. Conditional controls identify the option and value that enables them. ChangesMachine is true only for an implemented explicit execution control. These controls are advanced and off by default. The flag describes the control surface; it does not bypass fresh evidence, eligibility, journaling, verification or recovery requirements. Pass submitted key/value pairs to WorkflowOptionValidation.Validate . The validator fills omitted defaults, normalizes booleans and integers, and rejects unknown or duplicate keys, invalid choices, out-of-range or off-step sliders, oversized text, missing required values and conditional controls that are not active. A valid machine-changing request still authorizes nothing by itself. The operation-specific coordinator must collect fresh evidence and enforce its own execution boundary. After validation, WorkflowOptionProjection.CreateHost projects safe values into a WindowsWorkflowHost . It also adds a redacted Selected workflow options result so the normalized nondefault plan remains visible in reports and journals. Prepared System Restore settings drive the creation-readiness review. Selected filesystem repair settings drive exact-volume state collection, bounded read-only diagnosis and consequence review without authorizing or scheduling repair. Selected storage optimization settings drive exact-volume analysis. Selected printing targets correlate one exact queue and optional job with current bounded evidence without authorizing a queue or job change. A selected MSI product receives one exact repair review that combines current registration, Installer activity, restart state and administrator access while leaving execution with the guarded MSI coordinator. A selected Windows Time source is reviewed against the current role, policy ownership, local mode and peer configuration. A selected Event Log channel is reviewed against exact current configuration and policy ownership. Selected Firewall profiles are reviewed against complete ActiveStore state and policy ownership. A proposed computer name is reviewed against active and configured identity, role, restart state and administrator access. These reviews expose no repair identity. CreateServicingOptions projects the servicing monitor controls into ServicingMonitorOptions . Both reject invalid plans and every plan that requests a machine change. This gives CLI and embedded hosts one shared read-only execution path while leaving repair authorization with the operation-specific coordinators. The shared catalog currently includes desktop controls for Network, Audio, System Restore, Storage, Printing, Windows Installer, Windows Time, Event Log, Profiles, Computer Name, Background Transfers, App Packages, Installed Applications, Search, Component Cleanup, Windows Recovery, Firewall Services and BitLocker. Printing distinguishes queue repair, default selection and one blocked-job cancellation. Installer accepts only an optional normalized MSI product code. Time validates existing, domain and custom-peer choices. Event Log limits enablement to the four inspected diagnostic channels. Profile controls define the inactivity threshold and optional dependency review without authorizing profile changes. Computer Name requires one valid exact proposed name and keeps rename execution explicit. Background Transfers exposes running-account and administrator-only all-users inventory scopes without authorizing a job change. Package and application controls expose only the scopes implemented by their read-only collectors. Search rebuild, component cleanup and Windows Recovery enablement remain off by default and require their existing guarded coordinators. Firewall execution requires one or more exact profile selections. BitLocker validates mutually exclusive backup, encryption and protection-resume actions, including exact protector and recovery-file prerequisites. These definitions preserve each workflow's read-only default and represent execution as a separate advanced control. Capability folders Folder Responsibility"},{"u":"../engine/architecture.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Architecture","h":[["boundary","Boundary"],["project-relationships","Project relationships"],["internal-layers","Internal layers"],["windows-and-shell-modes","Windows and shell modes"],["failure-model","Failure model"]],"x":"Architecture The KillerBench.Engine is a UI-free project inside the KillerBench repository. The desktop, CLI, and tests reference it as separate hosts. Boundary The Engine owns: - Bounded Windows evidence collection - Deterministic interpretation and correlation - Workflow and recipe catalogs - Repair eligibility and execution coordination - Durable journals and no-repeat reconciliation - Report models and knowledge-pack readers The host owns: - Navigation, dialogs, controls, and presentation - User choices and consent - UAC relaunch behavior - Progress and cancellation controls - File pickers and report destinations - Product updates and deployment Project relationships The Engine does not reference WPF or desktop controls. Tests can substitute provider and repair hosts so parsers, eligibility, safety gates, interruption behavior, and recovery can be verified without changing the development computer. Internal layers Layer Responsibility --- --- Capability collectors Read bounded Windows state for one exact scope Evidence parsers Validate shape, identity, completeness, and limits Diagnostic interpretation Convert evidence into explicit workflow states Workflow orchestration Combine checks, progress, recipes, and journals Repair coordinators Recheck eligibility, prepare, mutate, verify, and reconcile Reports Present problems, actions, results, and limitations Knowledge packs Resolve versioned external reference data without changing logic Windows and shell modes The current native provider layer targets Windows and uses .NET, Windows APIs, PowerShell, and Windows command-line tools where appropriate. The Engine is not cross-platform today. A future Bash provider would be a separate host implementation behind explicit interfaces. It would need its own capability catalog, evidence contracts, safety rules, and tests. Translating PowerShell command strings into Bash would not create equivalent diagnostics. Failure model Collectors fail closed when identity is ambiguous, output is malformed, a limit is exceeded, or required authority cannot be inspected. Repair coordinators separate preview, preparation, dispatch, verification, and reconciliation so an interrupted process does not automatically repeat a machine change."},{"u":"../engine/knowledge-packs.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Knowledge packs","h":[["suitable-data","Suitable data"],["pack-contract","Pack contract"],["generators","Generators"],["updates","Updates"],["separation-from-diagnostics","Separation from diagnostics"]],"x":"Knowledge packs Knowledge packs keep large and frequently updated reference data outside the Engine's diagnostic logic. Suitable data - Windows and Setup error codes - Vendor and device identifiers - PCI, USB, ACPI, and UEFI references - Network OUI vendor data - Driver and support locations - Application and package metadata - Curated support links and diagnostic scopes Pack contract A pack should identify its format, schema version, source, source revision, generation time, and content hash. The Engine validates those fields before using the data. Unknown or invalid packs remain unavailable rather than silently falling back to guesses. Generators Generators belong in the repository when they are required to reproduce a distributed pack. They should: 1. Download only from named authoritative sources. 2. Record source revisions and licenses. 3. Validate input before transformation. 4. Normalize data deterministically. 5. Write a manifest and content hash. 6. Produce the same output from the same source input. Large generated databases and temporary downloads do not belong in the Engine source folder. The repository can contain small fixtures for parser and schema tests. Updates The desktop About surface can offer an Update databases action. An update should download to a temporary location, validate the manifest and hash, then replace the active pack atomically. A failed update must leave the prior valid pack available. Separation from diagnostics Knowledge data can explain an identifier or suggest a next diagnostic scope. It cannot convert a historical code into a current fault, choose a driver automatically, or authorize a repair. Current machine evidence remains the deciding input."}];
